WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.567 --> 00:00:05.167
-Hey, everybody, welcome back.

00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:07.834
So...

00:00:07.834 --> 00:00:10.968
Our last "Corrections"
was a real humdinger,

00:00:10.968 --> 00:00:12.868
if I do say so myself.

00:00:12.868 --> 00:00:16.200
Everything really you want,
you could hope for,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.767
out of an episode
of "Corrections."

00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:20.934
There was a problem, though,
the only problem, really.

00:00:20.934 --> 00:00:26.634
It snuck over
the 20-minute mark.

00:00:26.634 --> 00:00:32.167
And while that's not
an issue per se,

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:34.067
it does --

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:35.834
There's a risk.

00:00:35.834 --> 00:00:37.067
One of the stipulations

00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:42.567
for the Emmy category
of this show

00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:45.567
is that it averages
under 20 minutes.

00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:48.200
So we now, having crept over,

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.400
we're gonna have to get it back
somewhere else.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.801
It's just something
to keep our eye on,

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:55.968
because I think we can all agree

00:00:55.968 --> 00:01:01.634
the only way
we're not winning Emmy gold

00:01:01.634 --> 00:01:04.400
is due to
a [bleep] technicality.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.033
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.601 --> 00:01:13.033
What is the category --
the category has a weird name.

00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:17.133
Is it Best Dashed-Off
Thursday Bull[Bleep]

00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:21.067
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:22.767
I'm happy to announce
some of you might be sad,

00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:25.167
but we are now --
we have completed

00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:30.501
the E.B. White arc

00:01:30.501 --> 00:01:32.400
on "Corrections."

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.100
One thing we never
got to is what his initials

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.801
stand for, which, you know,

00:01:38.801 --> 00:01:41.033
obviously we did
a bunch of scholarship.

00:01:44.033 --> 00:01:48.367
The "B" stands for "B."

00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:50.067
It's just the "B" is the "B."
The "E" on the other hand --

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:52.167
Oh, sorry.

00:01:52.167 --> 00:01:54.968
We were talking
about the song "Stan,"

00:01:54.968 --> 00:01:58.300
and in referencing that song,

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:02.667
I said,
"I wrote the address perfect."

00:02:02.667 --> 00:02:04.067
It's -- you guys can't

00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:06.834
figure this part
of "Corrections" out.

00:02:06.834 --> 00:02:08.501
So many of you said,

00:02:08.501 --> 00:02:09.667
the correct grammar is --

00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:13.968
"I wrote
the address perfectly."

00:02:13.968 --> 00:02:17.067
I was quoting lyrics.

00:02:17.067 --> 00:02:19.567
So if you have
an issue with the grammar,

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:21.868
there are three people
you can take it up with --

00:02:21.868 --> 00:02:24.434
Eminem,

00:02:24.434 --> 00:02:26.501
Marshall Mathers...

00:02:26.501 --> 00:02:27.601
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.601 --> 00:02:29.634
...Slim Shady.

00:02:29.634 --> 00:02:32.601
Also, don't take it up with him.

00:02:32.601 --> 00:02:34.701
Okay?

00:02:34.701 --> 00:02:36.901
I mean, I'm in on this, right?

00:02:36.901 --> 00:02:38.434
"Ha, ha. You said that wrong."

00:02:38.434 --> 00:02:44.801
You think Eminem
wants corrections

00:02:44.801 --> 00:02:48.000
from the likes of you?

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.133
Oh, Slim Shady, please stand up.

00:02:50.133 --> 00:02:52.968
I guess we're ending our
sentences with prepositions now?

00:02:52.968 --> 00:02:55.000
[ Laughter ]

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.434
There was some criticism --
I'm not gonna --

00:03:00.434 --> 00:03:06.467
I said Stan
was the titular character

00:03:06.467 --> 00:03:07.734
in the song "Stan."

00:03:07.734 --> 00:03:11.467
And I was told it was --
he was the eponymous char--

00:03:11.467 --> 00:03:13.901
I think both are fine.

00:03:13.901 --> 00:03:17.167
I'm gonna die on that hill.

00:03:17.167 --> 00:03:18.834
You know, a theme
of "Corrections" recently

00:03:18.834 --> 00:03:22.501
has been, thanks
to the E.B. White saga,

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:24.300
which has completed...

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.868
[ Man chuckles ]

00:03:26.868 --> 00:03:28.567
...but the theme
was typewritten letters.

00:03:28.567 --> 00:03:30.067
And I was thinking,
you know, going back

00:03:30.067 --> 00:03:33.701
and watching the "Stan,"
hearing the track

00:03:33.701 --> 00:03:35.100
and watching the video,
it's very --

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.300
Eminem, again, a genius,
made a really good choice

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.868
to make them handwritten

00:03:38.868 --> 00:03:41.234
with a pencil
instead of typewritten,

00:03:41.234 --> 00:03:44.801
because the scratching of
the pencil underneath that track

00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:46.200
is very haunting.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.167
And I think that would not
have been the case

00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:52.200
had it been
a typewritten letter.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.534
And I wanted to prove it
so just take a listen,

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:55.968
see if you can
hear the difference.

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:57.400
-♪ You must not-a got'em ♪
[ Typewriter clacking ]

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.167
♪ There probably was a problem
at the post office ♪

00:03:59.167 --> 00:04:00.367
♪ Or something ♪

00:04:00.367 --> 00:04:01.734
♪ Sometimes
I scribble addresses ♪

00:04:01.734 --> 00:04:03.300
♪ Too sloppy when I jot them ♪

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.267
♪ Anyway, what's been up, man?
How's your daughter? ♪

00:04:06.267 --> 00:04:07.400
-Worse.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.534
[ Light laughter ]

00:04:12.501 --> 00:04:16.167
I loved the Super Bowl
halftime show.

00:04:16.167 --> 00:04:18.234
I did.

00:04:18.234 --> 00:04:20.601
Eminem, Kendrick,

00:04:20.601 --> 00:04:23.100
Mary J. Blige, Snoop,

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.767
50 Cent.

00:04:25.767 --> 00:04:27.067
I think that's everyone.

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:29.801
Ah! I forgot about Dre.

00:04:29.801 --> 00:04:32.701
[ Laughter ]

00:04:36.868 --> 00:04:40.400
I was just acting.
I didn't forget about Dre.

00:04:43.133 --> 00:04:44.834
You know what's the most
interesting thing about Eminem?

00:04:44.834 --> 00:04:46.801
Most rappers are Black,

00:04:46.801 --> 00:04:48.667
but Eminem Be White.

00:04:48.667 --> 00:04:50.234
That's his name.

00:04:50.234 --> 00:04:52.467
[ Light laughter ]

00:04:56.334 --> 00:04:57.968
[ Chuckles ]

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:04.834
That's a a good transition
to the Beastie Boys.

00:05:04.834 --> 00:05:06.200
I think about the Beastie Boys
all the time,

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.501
but I thought about them
a lot this week.

00:05:07.501 --> 00:05:10.234
I was telling this to Shoemaker.

00:05:10.234 --> 00:05:11.434
I thought about it
a lot this week

00:05:11.434 --> 00:05:14.834
because Kaley Cuoco
was on the show.

00:05:14.834 --> 00:05:16.400
And for whatever reason,
I just thought

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.400
if she was famous in 1989,

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.834
I'm pretty sure the Beastie Boys
would have had this line

00:05:22.834 --> 00:05:24.167
in one of their songs.

00:05:24.167 --> 00:05:27.934
♪ I got more rhymes
than Kaley Cuoco ♪

00:05:27.934 --> 00:05:29.767
These aren't really corrections
anymore.

00:05:29.767 --> 00:05:32.167
[ Laughter ]

00:05:37.501 --> 00:05:40.234
[ Sighs ]

00:05:40.234 --> 00:05:43.267
"Lord of the Rings."

00:05:43.267 --> 00:05:45.033
I feel like this
has gotten lost.

00:05:45.033 --> 00:05:47.100
I've tried to establish this
every time

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:51.534
I talk about those books,
those films.

00:05:51.534 --> 00:05:53.834
If you like them,
I'm so happy for you.

00:05:53.834 --> 00:05:56.434
I am a fan of many things.

00:05:56.434 --> 00:06:00.400
I think fandom
is a wonderful gift.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.467
It's not for me, but I'm not --

00:06:02.467 --> 00:06:06.100
I don't want to disabuse you
of your love of those things,

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:08.133
and I don't want to do anything
to make it seem like

00:06:08.133 --> 00:06:09.834
it's frivolous, because, again,
I'm sure you would find

00:06:09.834 --> 00:06:12.801
many of the things that I love
frivolous as well.

00:06:12.801 --> 00:06:17.033
And with that said, I feel like
I've been hard on "Rings,"

00:06:17.033 --> 00:06:20.667
and I do want to try to rectify
that a little today.

00:06:20.667 --> 00:06:23.334
I implied -- I said
last time we were here

00:06:23.334 --> 00:06:26.701
a couple weeks ago,
no major characters died

00:06:26.701 --> 00:06:29.167
in "The Lord of the Rings"
films.

00:06:29.167 --> 00:06:32.968
I forgot to mention
that a lot of people

00:06:32.968 --> 00:06:38.067
who saw the films died
of natural causes

00:06:38.067 --> 00:06:39.934
just due to, like, the length.

00:06:39.934 --> 00:06:41.868
[ Light laughter ]

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:46.934
Boromir --
that was the character

00:06:46.934 --> 00:06:48.100
the most people
were upset about.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:50.367
Boromir died, and...

00:06:52.734 --> 00:06:54.701
Boromir -- It was played --
not to be confused

00:06:54.701 --> 00:06:55.701
with Bordomir.

00:06:57.968 --> 00:07:00.434
Boromir was played by the great
British actor Sean Bean.

00:07:00.434 --> 00:07:03.467
Sean Bean, though, famously dies
in most of his movies

00:07:03.467 --> 00:07:07.634
and TV shows, so I feel like

00:07:07.634 --> 00:07:11.100
it's easier to overlook,
in my defense.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:13.734
This again --

00:07:13.734 --> 00:07:17.000
This is more like
an interesting thing I caught

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:18.834
more than a correction.

00:07:21.167 --> 00:07:22.701
I just want to clarify that,
like, you know,

00:07:22.701 --> 00:07:26.701
for the Emmy voters.
Like, I know.

00:07:26.701 --> 00:07:28.501
There was an interview
with Nic Cage --

00:07:28.501 --> 00:07:29.968
he's got this great movie
coming out

00:07:29.968 --> 00:07:34.200
I'm so excited to see --
where people said like,

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:36.734
"Hey, do you want to --
would you ever host 'SNL'?"

00:07:36.734 --> 00:07:38.234
And he said, "You know who
should host 'SNL'

00:07:38.234 --> 00:07:41.300
is Andy Samberg as me."

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:44.834
That's what he actually said.
This is -- you can look it up.

00:07:44.834 --> 00:07:46.801
And I -- you know, I obviously
have some --

00:07:46.801 --> 00:07:47.801
I think it's a great idea.

00:07:47.801 --> 00:07:49.901
I have some pull with Lorne,

00:07:49.901 --> 00:07:51.200
but I just --
first I want to make --

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:55.434
I want to even see
if Andy would be interested.

00:07:55.434 --> 00:07:59.033
And again, this is never --

00:07:59.033 --> 00:08:02.334
he never knows I'm calling,
so this could break either way.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:06.634
[ Line ringing ]

00:08:25.067 --> 00:08:28.334
Oh, he's not home.

00:08:28.334 --> 00:08:29.868
I'll keep trying, though,
and that's a fun thing

00:08:29.868 --> 00:08:31.534
to come back for.

00:08:31.534 --> 00:08:33.868
[ Laughter ]

00:08:39.167 --> 00:08:41.033
I have been --

00:08:41.033 --> 00:08:45.267
I haven't been as critical
of graphics recently.

00:08:45.267 --> 00:08:49.467
But we had this graphic
and "A Closer Look" on Monday.

00:08:49.467 --> 00:08:51.901
That's an eagle...

00:08:51.901 --> 00:08:53.601
smoking an e-cigarette

00:08:53.601 --> 00:08:55.367
while checking its e-mail.

00:08:57.501 --> 00:09:02.234
I'm gonna read you
the correction I got from this.

00:09:02.234 --> 00:09:05.234
"The eagle would be typing
with its talons, not its wing.

00:09:05.234 --> 00:09:06.934
E-cigarettes don't make
that much vapor.

00:09:06.934 --> 00:09:09.567
The glasses wouldn't fit on
the eagle since it has no ears.

00:09:09.567 --> 00:09:11.534
Eagles have the best eyesight
in the animal kingdom,

00:09:11.534 --> 00:09:13.300
so there's no way
an eagle would need glasses.

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:14.567
If the glasses
were not the eagle's

00:09:14.567 --> 00:09:16.501
but were facing him,
it implies the eagle

00:09:16.501 --> 00:09:19.033
just sat down at someone else's
chair in the middle of a cafe

00:09:19.033 --> 00:09:20.968
and usurped their computer
to check e-mails.

00:09:20.968 --> 00:09:22.467
Who does that?"

00:09:22.467 --> 00:09:24.868
[ Laughter ]

00:09:24.868 --> 00:09:26.767
"In what country are you allowed
to vape indoors

00:09:26.767 --> 00:09:29.901
in a cafe anyway?"

00:09:29.901 --> 00:09:31.133
All very fair.

00:09:31.133 --> 00:09:33.367
-I will say this is --

00:09:33.367 --> 00:09:34.868
I can take a look again.

00:09:34.868 --> 00:09:38.334
I didn't think this was a cafe.
I thought it was an office.

00:09:38.334 --> 00:09:39.834
He might be like,
"Eagles in an office?"

00:09:39.834 --> 00:09:42.067
But it's true. Sometimes there
are eagles in an office.

00:09:42.067 --> 00:09:45.133
Like, one time Donald Trump
had an eagle in his office.

00:09:45.133 --> 00:09:47.234
Take a look.

00:09:47.234 --> 00:09:48.434
-Oh.

00:09:48.434 --> 00:09:49.801
[ Laughter ]

00:09:49.801 --> 00:09:51.400
-And I didn't want to show that
because this isn't --

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:53.901
"Corrections" isn't political.

00:09:53.901 --> 00:09:56.167
That's "A Closer Look,"
and Sal, though --

00:09:56.167 --> 00:09:57.701
Sal was like,
"You gotta show the clip!"

00:09:57.701 --> 00:10:00.767
And I'm like, "Sal, leave
'Corrections' alone, okay?

00:10:00.767 --> 00:10:04.033
This isn't your bully pulpit."

00:10:04.033 --> 00:10:05.501
Because I gotta be hon--
I don't --

00:10:05.501 --> 00:10:08.167
a lot of that "Closer Look"
stuff is [bleep] crazy to me.

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:13.501
A lot of them I'm, like,
bullied into.

00:10:16.834 --> 00:10:18.434
And he's like,
"You gotta show --

00:10:18.434 --> 00:10:20.901
You gotta show the eagle video."

00:10:20.901 --> 00:10:23.033
I'm like, "It's weird."

00:10:23.033 --> 00:10:24.501
He's like, "You're complicit!"

00:10:24.501 --> 00:10:25.701
Always with that.

00:10:29.067 --> 00:10:30.968
Always.
But that's what --

00:10:30.968 --> 00:10:32.734
and one of the nice things
about "Corrections" is,

00:10:32.734 --> 00:10:36.033
because it's apolitical,
I think it's what

00:10:36.033 --> 00:10:37.701
maybe people on the other side
of the aisle,

00:10:37.701 --> 00:10:40.434
it's the part of our show
they engage with the most.

00:10:40.434 --> 00:10:42.167
Which is good because --
this is a little awkward --

00:10:42.167 --> 00:10:46.934
I have some corrections
for members of the right.

00:10:46.934 --> 00:10:49.167
This -- I always get in trouble
because we say

00:10:49.167 --> 00:10:52.701
that Trump hates windmills a lot
in "A Closer Look."

00:10:52.701 --> 00:10:55.100
It is --
he does hate wind turbines,

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:57.000
but he calls them windmills.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.467
So people are always like,
"Hey, man, you're talking about

00:10:59.467 --> 00:11:01.601
wind turbines."
Like, we know, but I --

00:11:01.601 --> 00:11:04.601
So hopefully if he's watching,
like, moving forward,

00:11:04.601 --> 00:11:07.200
you know, hate what you hate,
but that's --

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:09.033
you're saying it wrong.

00:11:10.767 --> 00:11:13.667
Oh, I also -- we talked about
how Ted Cruz was, you know,

00:11:13.667 --> 00:11:16.367
basically saying that Disney --

00:11:16.367 --> 00:11:19.667
Disney's rejection of Florida's
so-called "Don't Say Gay" bill

00:11:19.667 --> 00:11:21.234
means that we're just, like,
a step away

00:11:21.234 --> 00:11:24.567
from Mickey and Pluto
going at it.

00:11:24.567 --> 00:11:27.434
And we made a joke about
now if you go on Google,

00:11:27.434 --> 00:11:29.801
like, the autocomplete is,
you know,

00:11:29.801 --> 00:11:31.968
"They go on a picnic,
go for a drive,

00:11:31.968 --> 00:11:33.133
go to town on each other."

00:11:33.133 --> 00:11:35.434
And people said, "Actually,
Google weeds out

00:11:35.434 --> 00:11:37.567
suggestive phrases
in the autocomplete."

00:11:37.567 --> 00:11:39.834
But not right now.

00:11:39.834 --> 00:11:42.567
If you Google Mickey and Pluto,
thanks to Ted Cruz --

00:11:42.567 --> 00:11:44.434
thanks to conservative
Senator Ted Cruz --

00:11:44.434 --> 00:11:46.601
the number-one choice is --

00:11:46.601 --> 00:11:51.200
right now the three are,
"Mickey and Pluto going at it,"

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:53.634
"Mickey and Pluto
getting it on,"

00:11:53.634 --> 00:11:57.601
"Mickey and Pluto
varsity jacket."

00:11:57.601 --> 00:11:59.534
And I would just guess
that if you were think --

00:11:59.534 --> 00:12:01.501
if you were on the fence
about that varsity jacket

00:12:01.501 --> 00:12:04.534
and saw those first two,

00:12:04.534 --> 00:12:06.267
might not want any more.
Samberg texted me.

00:12:06.267 --> 00:12:08.100
Let's see what he has to say.
Hold on.

00:12:10.367 --> 00:12:12.000
Mm.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:13.934
Oh, he says,
"I'm in an edit meeting.

00:12:13.934 --> 00:12:15.367
Did I miss 'Corrections'?"

00:12:22.567 --> 00:12:23.934
I'm gonna try again.

00:12:23.934 --> 00:12:25.801
"Edit meeting." He's always
like, "I'm in showbiz."

00:12:25.801 --> 00:12:28.133
[ Line ringing ]

00:12:31.501 --> 00:12:34.667
Bad day for him not to answer,
what with this time issue.

00:12:34.667 --> 00:12:38.300
[ Line ringing ]

00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:40.434
[ Cellphone dings ]

00:12:40.434 --> 00:12:42.067
He just -- while I was
calling him, he texted,

00:12:42.067 --> 00:12:43.934
"I'm still in the meeting."

00:12:47.667 --> 00:12:50.167
[ Sputters ]

00:12:50.167 --> 00:12:52.300
All right.

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:53.767
[ Laughing ]
And then he wrote --

00:12:53.767 --> 00:12:56.901
'cause this ass [bleep]
every day --

00:12:56.901 --> 00:12:58.767
he does Queen Bee every day.

00:12:58.767 --> 00:13:01.033
That's him. See?
Like, he writes -- He gets a --

00:13:01.033 --> 00:13:02.701
Wait. Is that -- yeah, can you
see it? There you go.

00:13:02.701 --> 00:13:04.300
She gets Queen Bee every day

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:07.167
and then texts me
that he got it.

00:13:07.167 --> 00:13:08.934
And so he wrote,
"In edit meeting.

00:13:08.934 --> 00:13:10.167
Did I miss 'Corrections'?"

00:13:10.167 --> 00:13:11.400
I wrote, "Still going."
And then I'm like,

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:12.467
what am I gonna do,
wait for him to --

00:13:12.467 --> 00:13:13.634
I'll just call him.
He'll answer.

00:13:13.634 --> 00:13:14.968
And he wrote,
"I'm still in the meeting.

00:13:14.968 --> 00:13:17.000
Tell them I got Queen Bee."

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:20.000
[ Laughter ]

00:13:24.534 --> 00:13:27.934
Oh, now a -- a quick, uh,

00:13:27.934 --> 00:13:30.667
correction for...

00:13:30.667 --> 00:13:32.701
Tucker Carlson did a special
about manhood,

00:13:32.701 --> 00:13:34.601
and I guess there's a lot
about tanning your testicles.

00:13:34.601 --> 00:13:36.667
A few people reached out, said,
you can't turn your testicles.

00:13:36.667 --> 00:13:39.267
You can only tan your scrotum.

00:13:39.267 --> 00:13:41.067
That's a really good point.

00:13:41.067 --> 00:13:43.501
[ Laughter ]

00:13:43.501 --> 00:13:46.300
No one's like, "I'm gonna get
these kidneys golden brown."

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:48.167
[ Laughter ]

00:13:48.167 --> 00:13:49.901
That's a Tuck-up.

00:13:49.901 --> 00:13:52.367
[ Laughter ]

00:13:55.767 --> 00:13:58.567
Um...

00:13:58.834 --> 00:14:03.067
We said -- We made a joke about
microwaving your nutsack,

00:14:03.067 --> 00:14:06.834
and more people than you would
think pointed out you can't --

00:14:06.834 --> 00:14:09.267
the microwave only works
if you fully close the door,

00:14:09.267 --> 00:14:12.000
so you can't...
microwave your nutsack.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:14.234
So apologies to everybody
who was, you know,

00:14:14.234 --> 00:14:16.934
looking forward to that.

00:14:16.934 --> 00:14:18.000
And I guess we mentioned
something else

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:19.067
that a person wrote me,

00:14:19.067 --> 00:14:21.634
"Bald eagles don't have
nutsacks.

00:14:21.634 --> 00:14:22.968
They have cloacas."

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:29.567
And a way to remember that is,

00:14:29.567 --> 00:14:32.067
in "Corrections,"

00:14:32.067 --> 00:14:35.534
we fix our "mistakas."

00:14:35.534 --> 00:14:38.400
♪ I got more balls
than an Eagles cloaca ♪

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:40.734
[ Light laughter ]

00:14:43.767 --> 00:14:47.901
I guess I pronounce
the "t" in "often,"

00:14:47.901 --> 00:14:51.300
which was very jarring
to people's ears.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:54.200
And if you are someone
that bothered,

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:57.033
you can [bleep] right "oft."

00:14:57.033 --> 00:14:59.267
[ Laughter ]

00:15:01.734 --> 00:15:03.734
I am in very good mood today.

00:15:03.734 --> 00:15:07.200
I got to go to my son's school
for the very first time today.

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:09.234
Because of COVID, I've never
been able to go inside.

00:15:09.234 --> 00:15:12.334
He did this family tree thing,
and I went in.

00:15:12.334 --> 00:15:13.834
It was very cute.

00:15:13.834 --> 00:15:15.133
He had pictures
of everybody's family

00:15:15.133 --> 00:15:16.667
and he described them all.

00:15:16.667 --> 00:15:19.234
And one, it was just
cool to see your kid, like,

00:15:19.234 --> 00:15:21.367
you know, talk to his class
and have confidence.

00:15:21.367 --> 00:15:24.367
But the craziest thing was
at the end there was a Q&amp;A,

00:15:24.367 --> 00:15:28.234
and they said, "Do any of the
kids have questions for Ashe?"

00:15:28.234 --> 00:15:32.467
And one of the kids asked him,
"How's Stefon?"

00:15:32.467 --> 00:15:34.901
[ Light laughter ]

00:15:36.767 --> 00:15:38.400
The mug.
We're working on the mug.

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:40.067
We don't have it yet.

00:15:40.067 --> 00:15:42.100
We went to
the NBC merchandise people.

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:44.400
They're a little backed up.

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:46.501
They don't turn it around
super fast

00:15:46.501 --> 00:15:48.300
when you have new merch.

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:51.868
I went down, you know,
to try to rattle the cage,

00:15:51.868 --> 00:15:53.601
tried to get things sped up,
because obviously

00:15:53.601 --> 00:15:56.701
I want you guys to be able
to buy this "Corrections,"

00:15:56.701 --> 00:15:58.033
you know, the jackal mug.

00:15:58.033 --> 00:16:00.434
And I went down.
It was a ghost town.

00:16:00.434 --> 00:16:03.367
There was one guy wrapped up
in, like, a "Smash" blanket,

00:16:03.367 --> 00:16:05.267
and...

00:16:05.267 --> 00:16:07.801
[ Laughter ]

00:16:07.801 --> 00:16:09.367
...he was like, "Oh!"
Like, he was sound asleep.

00:16:09.367 --> 00:16:10.300
He's like, "Who are you?!"

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:11.901
And I was like,
"I'm Seth Meyers."

00:16:11.901 --> 00:16:12.834
He's like, "From 'SNL'?"

00:16:12.834 --> 00:16:14.868
And I'm like, "No, I have
the 'Late Night' show now.

00:16:14.868 --> 00:16:16.834
He goes, "Well, I thought
that was Jimmy Fallon."

00:16:16.834 --> 00:16:19.400
And I was like, "No, he's got --
he's 'The Tonight Show.'"

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:20.667
And he's like,
"What happened to Jay Leno?"

00:16:20.667 --> 00:16:23.634
And I'm like, "He retired,"
and he's like, "Oh, no!"

00:16:23.634 --> 00:16:25.701
[ Laughter ]

00:16:25.701 --> 00:16:29.767
He goes, "We just finished
the "JayWalking" Zune case.

00:16:29.767 --> 00:16:32.000
[ Laughter ]

00:16:38.367 --> 00:16:41.467
We talked about a brontosaurus.
I said brontosaurus.

00:16:41.467 --> 00:16:43.300
People say, actually,
it's the Apatosaurus.

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:45.033
But then we decided
both are acceptable.

00:16:45.033 --> 00:16:47.767
And then I ventured that
if we were, you know --

00:16:47.767 --> 00:16:50.801
if we were allowed to sort of
change dinosaur names,

00:16:50.801 --> 00:16:52.634
or agree on
a new dinosaur name,

00:16:52.634 --> 00:16:55.868
maybe it was time to drop
the "p" in "pterodactyl."

00:16:55.868 --> 00:16:57.901
Now, I was told
you couldn't do that

00:16:57.901 --> 00:17:02.133
because "pter" -- p-t-e-r --
that means "wing."

00:17:02.133 --> 00:17:06.634
"Dactyl" means "finger,"
and "ter" without the "p" --

00:17:06.634 --> 00:17:09.734
"ter" means "earth,"
so it wouldn't be the same.

00:17:09.734 --> 00:17:14.267
So just a reminder that
pterodactyl with a "p"

00:17:14.267 --> 00:17:16.200
is a wing.

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:17.267
"Terodactyl" with a "t"
is earth.

00:17:17.267 --> 00:17:20.200
So if you see a flying dinosaur,
that's a pterodactyl.

00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:23.200
And if you see one walking on
the earth, that's terodactyl.

00:17:25.634 --> 00:17:29.367
Mentioned Ben Warheit making
a mistake in one of his pieces.

00:17:29.367 --> 00:17:34.167
And someone said, that's crazy
that he would make a mistake

00:17:34.167 --> 00:17:37.834
because "Warheit"
means "truth" in German.

00:17:37.834 --> 00:17:41.934
But in English it means
"hates to proofread."

00:17:41.934 --> 00:17:43.667
[ Laughter ]

00:17:46.534 --> 00:17:48.534
People have asked
how you spell Shoemaker.

00:17:48.534 --> 00:17:50.067
Is it like Shoemaker
in the [indistinct],

00:17:50.067 --> 00:17:53.033
or Schumacher -- is it spelled
like the German racecar driver?

00:17:53.033 --> 00:17:54.634
This is also easy to remember

00:17:54.634 --> 00:17:59.968
because Michael Schumacher
is one of the fastest --

00:17:59.968 --> 00:18:03.400
traveled faster than almost
any human on Earth ever,

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:06.868
whereas Shoemaker is
on week four of using a cane

00:18:06.868 --> 00:18:09.267
and somehow getting slower.

00:18:09.267 --> 00:18:11.067
[ Laughter ]

00:18:13.968 --> 00:18:19.734
Watching him move down a hallway

00:18:19.734 --> 00:18:23.234
makes "Lord of the Rings" feel
like a Mountain Dew commercial.

00:18:23.234 --> 00:18:25.834
[ Laughter ]

00:18:33.367 --> 00:18:34.801
One piece of viewer mail.

00:18:34.801 --> 00:18:36.434
"Hey, Seth, I noticed
you had some trouble

00:18:36.434 --> 00:18:37.400
putting on your gloves.

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:39.234
I've been a lifeguard instructor
for many years,

00:18:39.234 --> 00:18:40.801
and I find the easiest
and quickest way

00:18:40.801 --> 00:18:42.467
to put on a pair of gloves
like that,

00:18:42.467 --> 00:18:43.934
at least on a pool deck,

00:18:43.934 --> 00:18:46.300
is to first fill them with water
and put your hand in,

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:47.834
letting your hands
displace the water.

00:18:47.834 --> 00:18:49.567
I don't know if it'll help you
in your situation,

00:18:49.567 --> 00:18:51.534
but lifeguard students
have a timed skill

00:18:51.534 --> 00:18:52.801
that involves putting on gloves,

00:18:52.801 --> 00:18:54.200
and I wanted to
pass that along."

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:56.334
Why do you put on gloves?

00:18:56.334 --> 00:18:58.601
[ Laughter ]

00:19:02.267 --> 00:19:03.868
Uh, I just want to say --

00:19:03.868 --> 00:19:05.267
hopefully this is
legally binding --

00:19:05.267 --> 00:19:09.767
if you're a lifeguard
and I ever need CPR, skip it.

00:19:09.767 --> 00:19:11.934
[ Light laughter ]

00:19:13.834 --> 00:19:17.267
This is a book update.

00:19:17.267 --> 00:19:19.334
It was really cool.

00:19:19.334 --> 00:19:22.734
"The New York Times"
sent this over.

00:19:22.734 --> 00:19:27.367
And they made this, obviously.
It's pretty cool.

00:19:27.367 --> 00:19:29.934
It's a way to track
how your book is doing.

00:19:29.934 --> 00:19:32.234
And I want to --
I have some exciting --

00:19:32.234 --> 00:19:33.601
this is week five.

00:19:33.601 --> 00:19:36.667
So week one,
we were number three.

00:19:36.667 --> 00:19:38.801
Okay, that's cool.

00:19:38.801 --> 00:19:40.467
And then we were...

00:19:42.701 --> 00:19:45.167
...number four in week two.

00:19:45.167 --> 00:19:46.934
Okay. Here we go, like that.

00:19:46.934 --> 00:19:49.100
It's weird that they didn't know
what size to make these.

00:19:50.534 --> 00:19:52.701
And then where do we end up?

00:19:54.701 --> 00:19:59.334
Week three, we were number six.

00:19:59.334 --> 00:20:00.868
This is bad.
I mean, it's so nice

00:20:00.868 --> 00:20:01.934
for "The New York Times"
to send this over,

00:20:01.934 --> 00:20:03.601
but this is embarrassing and I'm
sorry I'm doing this, then.

00:20:03.601 --> 00:20:05.434
They -- the back --
they obviously didn't --

00:20:05.434 --> 00:20:07.501
they got their --
they eyeballed it wrong.

00:20:07.501 --> 00:20:12.200
[ Laughter ]

00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:13.934
Paper of record?

00:20:16.567 --> 00:20:18.501
And then it was number eight.

00:20:19.567 --> 00:20:22.033
And then this is --
you might notice a trend,

00:20:22.033 --> 00:20:25.167
which is why this is going to
surprise you guys.

00:20:26.767 --> 00:20:29.033
Week five...

00:20:29.033 --> 00:20:30.667
it's number nine.

00:20:32.133 --> 00:20:34.834
Let me say something.

00:20:34.834 --> 00:20:36.901
Lot of Easter books on the list.

00:20:39.701 --> 00:20:41.968
And the gravy train
just left town...

00:20:45.234 --> 00:20:46.234
...for the Easter books.

00:20:46.234 --> 00:20:49.601
Also, if you bought
a Easter book last week,

00:20:49.601 --> 00:20:50.634
that's like the way you say,
like,

00:20:50.634 --> 00:20:53.567
"I forgot to get you a basket."

00:20:55.567 --> 00:20:57.968
How'd we do on time,
"Schumacher"?

00:20:59.234 --> 00:21:01.567
-20--
-Ah, [bleep]. 20?

00:21:02.367 --> 00:21:03.434
-21.

00:21:03.434 --> 00:21:05.467
-[ Sputters ]

00:21:07.501 --> 00:21:09.100
Maybe...

00:21:12.167 --> 00:21:13.634
...cut the Beastie Boys stuff?

